<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav signed_in?={@signed_in?} current={:compare} />

  <section class="py-16 sm:py-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-400">Compare</p>
        <.marketing_heading tag="h1" scale={:hero} class="mt-2">
          Custom MCP server vs emisar
        </.marketing_heading>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-8 text-zinc-400 text-pretty">
          A custom MCP server makes sense when you need a small adapter over an API that already
          handles authorization and audit. emisar fits ongoing agent work across real hosts,
          through actions you can review and control. The decision is how much of that execution
          system your team wants to build and maintain.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-16 max-w-3xl">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-zinc-50 text-balance">
          A tool handler is the beginning
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400 text-pretty">
          Connecting a model's request to a command is the first step. You also need to check
          who can call it and which inputs are valid, handle lost connections, and record the
          result. Those checks must keep working as you add hosts, clients, and actions.
        </p>
      </div>

      <%!-- The difference, shown: the same psql, two ways. Amber = the handler
           in a minimal example; emerald = the
           declared pack action. Same terminal look as /compare/raw-ssh. --%>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:gap-5">
        <div class="min-w-0 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-amber-500/25 bg-black/40">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-amber-500/20 bg-amber-500/5 px-4 py-2.5">
            <span class="h-2 w-2 rounded-full bg-amber-500/70"></span>
            <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-amber-200">
              your-mcp-server.ts · simplified example
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-1.5 px-4 py-4 font-mono text-[11.5px] leading-relaxed">
            <p class="text-zinc-300">
              server.tool("pg_kill", async (&#123; pid &#125;) => &#123;
            </p>
            <p class="text-zinc-300">&nbsp;&nbsp;return ssh.exec(</p>
            <p class="text-zinc-300">
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;`psql -c "…terminate_backend($&#123;pid&#125;)"`)
            </p>
            <p class="text-zinc-300">&#125;)</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-amber-300">// then everything you still own:</p>
            <p class="text-zinc-500">//&nbsp;&nbsp;validate pid · redact secrets</p>
            <p class="text-zinc-500">//&nbsp;&nbsp;risk gate → human approval</p>
            <p class="text-zinc-500">//&nbsp;&nbsp;audit: who · what · why</p>
            <p class="text-zinc-500">//&nbsp;&nbsp;reconnect · orphans · per-user scope</p>
            <p class="text-zinc-500">//&nbsp;&nbsp;OAuth + transport per client</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="min-w-0 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-brand-500/25 bg-black/40">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-brand-500/20 bg-brand-500/5 px-4 py-2.5">
            <span class="h-2 w-2 rounded-full bg-brand-500"></span>
            <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-200">postgres.yml · emisar pack action</span>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-1.5 px-4 py-4 font-mono text-[11.5px] leading-relaxed text-zinc-300">
            <p>id: postgres.terminate_backend</p>
            <p>kind: exec</p>
            <p>
              risk: high&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text-zinc-500"># → policy gates it</span>
            </p>
            <p>args:</p>
            <p>&nbsp;&nbsp;- name: pid</p>
            <p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;type: integer</p>
            <p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;required: true</p>
            <p>execution:</p>
            <p>&nbsp;&nbsp;command:</p>
            <p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;binary: psql</p>
            <p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;argv: ["-tAc", "SELECT</p>
            <p>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;pg_terminate_backend(&#123;&#123; args.pid &#125;&#125;)"]
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400 text-pretty">
        Both run the same query. The left drops an unvalidated
        <code class="text-zinc-300">$&#123;pid&#125;</code>
        straight into a SQL string, one slip from injection. The right declares
        <code class="text-zinc-300">pid</code>
        an integer that the runner re-validates before <code class="text-zinc-300">psql</code>
        ever sees it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-y border-zinc-900 bg-black/20 py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-zinc-50 text-balance">
          What you'd build and own
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400 text-pretty">
          You can build these controls yourself. Your team then owns their tests, updates,
          and behavior when something fails.
        </p>
      </div>
      <%!-- One data list, two layouts: a table on md+, stacked cards on mobile, so
        the 3-way comparison reads without a horizontal scroll on phones. --%>
      <% rows = [
        {"Argument validation",
         "Reject undeclared args, coerce types, enforce bounds, and do all of it on the host, since the model's output is untrusted",
         "Typed schemas revalidated on the host; the executable and any shell program are fixed by the reviewed pack"},
        {"Tool integrity", "Your deploy pipeline is the only thing stopping a changed handler",
         "Content-addressed packs; published hashes auto-pin, and a changed or unrecognized SHA-256 blocks dispatch until an admin reviews it"},
        {"Host transport", "Credentials and routing you design and secure",
         "Outbound-only runners, single-use-by-default enrollment, and revocable per-runner tokens; the runner exposes no inbound listener"},
        {"Policy & approval", "Conditional logic in code plus a homemade approval queue",
         "Risk-tier defaults + per-action overrides, human approval, and standing grants that expire"},
        {"Per-user access", "Hand-rolled, and easy to get subtly wrong",
         "Per-member runner scopes in UI, API, and dispatch; keys inherit the creator's scope at call time"},
        {"Multi-client access",
         "Auth and transport work per client (Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor, CLIs)",
         "OAuth 2.1 remote MCP plus a stdio bridge, one catalog behind both"},
        {"Output safety", "Your redaction regexes, your output caps, your bug if one leaks",
         "20 built-in patterns plus your own, masked on the host before egress; output bytes clamped"},
        {"Audit", "Logs you design, store, and have to prove weren't edited",
         "Hash-chained host journal (emisar audit verify), searchable cloud audit, NDJSON SIEM export"},
        {"Failure modes", "You handle reconnects, orphaned runs, and leaked child processes",
         "Run-state reconciled on reconnect; a dropped socket keeps the run alive and replays its result; children reaped on cancel and timeout"}
      ] %>

      <div class="mt-5 hidden overflow-hidden rounded-lg ring-1 ring-white/10 md:block">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-black/40 text-xs text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="w-[22%] px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">Concern</th>
              <th class="w-[39%] px-4 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-amber-200">
                You build &amp; maintain it
              </th>
              <th class="w-[39%] px-4 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-brand-300">
                emisar ships it
              </th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 bg-zinc-950/70">
            <tr :for={{concern, diy, emisar} <- rows}>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top font-medium text-zinc-100">{concern}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top leading-6 text-zinc-400">{diy}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top leading-6 text-zinc-300">{emisar}</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-5 overflow-hidden rounded-lg bg-zinc-950/70 ring-1 ring-white/10 md:hidden">
        <div
          :for={{concern, diy, emisar} <- rows}
          class="border-b border-zinc-800 p-4 last:border-b-0"
        >
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">{concern}</p>
          <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-sm leading-6">
            <div>
              <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-amber-300">You build it</p>
              <p class="mt-1 text-zinc-400">{diy}</p>
            </div>
            <div class="border-l-2 border-brand-500/40 pl-3">
              <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-400">emisar</p>
              <p class="mt-1 text-zinc-300">{emisar}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="py-16 sm:py-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-zinc-50 text-balance">
          What emisar costs you
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-5xl divide-y divide-zinc-900 border-y border-zinc-900">
        <div class="py-5 sm:grid sm:grid-cols-[14rem_1fr] sm:gap-8">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">It's a vendor in your path.</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400 text-pretty sm:mt-0">
            We host the control plane.
            Your runner connects from your host and keeps its own journal. You can block
            actions on the runner even if the cloud allows them, but each request still
            goes through our service.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="py-5 sm:grid sm:grid-cols-[14rem_1fr] sm:gap-8">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">It's opinionated.</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400 text-pretty sm:mt-0">
            Actions are YAML packs, the policy model is risk tiers plus overrides, and the transport
            is MCP. If your workflow doesn't fit that shape, you are better off with a server you
            control than bending the workflow to fit ours.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="py-5 sm:grid sm:grid-cols-[14rem_1fr] sm:gap-8">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">You choose the available actions.</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400 text-pretty sm:mt-0">
            Use published packs or add your own commands as custom actions. For an agent
            that needs an unrestricted shell, see the <.link
              navigate={~p"/compare/raw-ssh-for-ai"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >SSH comparison</.link>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="py-5 sm:grid sm:grid-cols-[14rem_1fr] sm:gap-8">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">It's another bill.</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400 text-pretty sm:mt-0">
            Team is $20 per runner per month.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="py-16 sm:py-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[0.72fr_1.28fr]">
        <div class="pb-10 lg:pb-0 lg:pr-12">
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-zinc-100 text-balance">
            Build it yourself when
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-md text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400 text-pretty">
            A small MCP adapter can be enough for one team's workflow, especially when the API
            already handles access checks and audit records. It is a good fit when you don't
            expect to add more hosts or clients.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border-t border-zinc-800 pt-10 lg:border-l lg:border-t-0 lg:pl-12 lg:pt-0">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-zinc-50 text-balance">Reach for emisar when</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400 text-pretty">
            Connect your agent once, then add capabilities as packs. Use the published catalog
            for common infrastructure and wrap your own procedures as custom actions. Emisar
            handles the shared access checks, execution, approvals, and records as your hosts
            and workflows grow. You don't have to rebuild all of that and, which takes even more time,
            maintain it as the environment evolves.
          </p>

          <div class="mt-8 max-w-2xl rounded-xl border border-brand-900/40 bg-brand-950/20 p-5 text-sm text-zinc-300">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Try one task before choosing</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-6">
              Use the
              <.link navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">quickstart</.link>
              to connect a runner and run <code>linux.uptime</code>. Inspect the result and its record,
              then choose a relevant action from the <.link
                navigate={~p"/packs"}
                class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
              >published packs</.link>.
              For a longer example, the
              <.link
                navigate={~p"/use-cases/csi-data-loss"}
                class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
              >CSI data-loss investigation</.link>
              shows where the agent kept working and where a person had to decide.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <nav
        aria-label="Continue comparing emisar"
        class="mt-12 flex flex-col flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-8 gap-y-1 border-t border-zinc-900 pt-6 text-center text-sm sm:flex-row"
      >
        <.cta_link navigate={~p"/docs/security-model"} class="min-h-10 py-2.5">How the trust boundary works</.cta_link>
        <.cta_link tone={:muted} navigate={~p"/docs/action-packs"} class="min-h-10 py-2.5">The pack format</.cta_link>
        <.cta_link tone={:muted} navigate={~p"/compare/raw-ssh-for-ai"} class="min-h-10 py-2.5">Compare with raw SSH</.cta_link>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>

  <.marketing_cta
    headline="Connect your infrastructure in 5 minutes."
    subcopy="Start with ready-made packs and add your own actions through the same MCP server."
    secondary_label="Follow the quickstart"
    secondary_path={~p"/docs/quickstart"}
  />

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
